  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 108 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday:\n\nLong wave charts indicate that an upper-level ridge will be \npositioned over the region during the midweek period. Ensemble \nclusters are consistent in bringing an upper-level short wave \ntrough out of the western U.S. and into the northern and central\nPlains on Wednesday before reaching the western Great Lakes \nearly Thursday. This feature will likely be shearing with time \ngiven that it will encounter the long wave ridge.\n\nAt the surface, a sprawling area of high pressure will remain\nentrenched over the eastern U.S. That aforementioned short wave\ntrough will be reflected at the surface in the form of a cold\nfront. However, the track and shearing nature of the wave will\nlikely result in that front either remaining north of the area\nor being very weak in nature if it can manage to pass through\nthe Ozarks.\n\nGiven an overall lack of forcing and limited moisture return,\nit will be difficult to realize any measurable precipitation. We\nhave kept PoPs in the 10-20% range from Wednesday night into\nThursday if forcing and moisture return are on the higher end of\nthe ensemble envelope.\n\nNBM probabilistic data reveals tight clustering with respect to\ntemperatures for the midweek period, thus our confidence is high\nin temperature forecasts. High temperatures will return to the\nmiddle 60s to lower 70s by Thursday.\n\nFriday into Monday:\n\nEnsembles indicate a stronger upper level short wave trough\nemerging from the Rockies and into the central U.S. However, the\ntiming and magnitude of this wave vary considerably. We are\nconfident that this wave will be stronger than the feature that\npasses through the region around midweek as the long wave ridge\nis forecast to weaken over the central U.S. by the weekend.\n\nWith that being said, a cold front will likely pass through the\narea sometime in the Friday night or Saturday time frame.\nStronger forcing and better moisture return will both be favored\nwith this setup, thus we expect the potential for showers and\nperhaps a few thunderstorms with the frontal passage. The\npotential for thunder will depend on the quality of moisture\nreturn and overall instability. For now, we have PoPs in the\n30-50% range from late Friday night into Saturday night. PoPs\nmay be increased for a specific time period once we get a better\nfeel for the timing of that cold front.\n\nTemperatures ahead of that front will continue to be mild on\nFriday with highs ranging from the upper 60s to middle 70s.\nTemperatures will then cool off behind the front, however the\nmagnitude of cooling behind the front for Sunday and early next\nweek is still in question:\n\nScenario 1 (60% chance): Ensemble clusters indicate \nnorthwesterly flow developing behind the trough with a stronger \npolar fetch. This would result in a more substantial cooldown \nwith highs in the upper 40s to middle 50s and lows in the upper \n20s to middle 30s.\n\nScenario 2 (40% chance): Ensemble clusters indicate a flatter \nflow behind the passing trough and less in the way of a polar \nfetch. This would result in a more modest cooldown with highs \nfalling back into the upper 50s to middle 60s with lows in the \nmiddle 30s to lower 40s.",
